# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1 has "desirecounts → compromise" and "nodistinguish ∧ notcoincident ∧ compromise → ¬expresses"; sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1.2 has "compromise → ¬expresses" and "nodistinguish ∧ notcoincident → ¬expresses"; they differ when only "the common estimate does not distinguish between objects of natural desire and sources of experienced pleasure", "objects of natural desire and sources of experienced pleasure are not exactly coincident" and "the current estimate of the desirability of objects of pursuit can be taken to express simply men's experience of pleasure and pain" are true, which the first allows; sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1.3 has "nodistinguish ∧ notcoincident ∧ compromise → ¬expresses"
sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1 takes for granted "if men are apt to think desirable what they strongly desire, whether or not they have found it conducive to happiness on the whole, then the common opinion tends to represent a compromise between the average force of desires and the average experience of the consequences of gratifying them"; sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1.2 and sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1.3 do not
sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if men are apt to think desirable what they strongly desire, whether or not they have found it conducive to happiness on the whole, then the common opinion tends to represent a compromise between the average force of desires and the average experience of the consequences of gratifying them"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: U2: sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1 and sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1.2 take for granted "if (the common estimate does not distinguish between objects of natural desire and sources of experienced pleasure and objects of natural desire and sources of experienced pleasure are not exactly coincident), then it is not so that the current estimate of the desirability of objects of pursuit can be taken to express simply men's experience of pleasure and pain"; sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1.3 does not
sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1 and sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (the common estimate does not distinguish between objects of natural desire and sources of experienced pleasure and objects of natural desire and sources of experienced pleasure are not exactly coincident), then it is not so that the current estimate of the desirability of objects of pursuit can be taken to express simply men's experience of pleasure and pain"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1: argue/verdicts: valid as sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1, sidgwick-methods-2.logic.r1.2 read it, not as the others do: which reading is right decides the verdict
